  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe core idea is to use a multi-stage optimization protocol combining a structured initial guess with global and local optimization steps. The structured initial guess ensures a good starting point while global search capability prevents premature convergence. The main bottleneck addressed is the tendency of local optimizers to get stuck in suboptimal regions. This approach leverages the problem's structure by using a custom initial pattern and refining it with both global and local search heuristics. The use of domain-specific initial constructions ensures feasibility from the start and reduces the risk of invalid solutions during optimization.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nThe function h(x) is represented as an array of n_points samples over [0, 2], with the constraint sum(h) = n_points/2. The objective function is defined as:\nC\u2085 = max_k ( np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\") * dx )\n\nThe key mathematical formulation is the overlap integral for all possible shifts k \u2208 [0, 2]. This evaluates the same inner product as the correlation operation scaled by dx = 2/n_points. The problem is nonconvex due to the max function over lags, necessitating global search capabilities. The constraints are:\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n- sum(h) = n_points/2\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nStage 1: Parent validation and initialization\n- Verify that initial_h_values meets sum(h) = n_points/2 and 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n- Construct a regular initial guess when no parent is available: distribute 1s evenly at midpoints between grid points\n\nStage 2: Global optimization with adaptive mutation\n- Use a modified differential evolution algorithm with population size 50\n- Mutation factor: 0.8, crossover probability: 0.9\n- Track the best candidate from each generation\n- After each mutation, rescale the candidate to maintain sum(h) = n_points/2\n- Terminate when 200 generations complete or budget is reached\n\nStage 3: Local refinement with L-BFGS-B\n- Perform gradient-based optimization on the best DE candidate\n- Maintain sum constraint via rescaling after each iteration\n- Use restarts if objective plateaus\n\nStage 4: Escape local optima\n- After every 50 generations, perturb the best candidate with Gaussian noise\n- Clip values to [0, 1] and rescale to maintain constraints\n- Use a diminishing perturbation amplitude over time\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nDefault configuration:\n- n_points = 400 (balance accuracy and computational cost)\n- DE population size: 50\n- Mutation factor: 0.8\n- Crossover probability: 0.9\n- 200 generations\n- L-BFGS-B iterations: 100\n\nVariations:\n- For n_points \u2264 300: Increase DE population to 75 for better exploration\n- For n_points \u2265 600: Reduce DE population to 30 for speed\n- During local refinement: Use a restart if the objective improves by less than 0.001\n- When budget is low: Skip the local refinement stage\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nDominant cost: O(n_points\u00b2) correlation evaluation + O(n_points) rescaling. With n_points=400, this is 160,000 operations per DE evaluation. At 400 generations, this equals 64 million operations. Python's optimized numpy operations should complete this within 600 seconds.\n\nTermination criteria:\n- After 200 generations of DE\n- If budget is exhausted\n- If objective improvement per generation < 1e-5 for 5 consecutive generations\n\nRobustness measures:\n- Always clip and rescale to maintain constraints\n- Use a fallback to the initial guess if no valid solution is found\n- Restart optimization if objective becomes unbounded\n- Implement numerical stability checks for all operations\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFeasibility checks:\n- Verify sum(h) \u2248 n_points/2 with absolute tolerance 1e-6\n- Confirm 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n- Re-evaluate C\u2085 using the exact objective function\n\nSanity checks:\n- Validate that the initial guess meets constraints\n- Confirm that scaling maintains the sum\n- Ensure correlation computation uses correct dx = 2.0/n_points\n- Verify that all operations preserve the 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 bounds\n\nRe-evaluation protocol:\n- Recompute C\u2085 using the original objective function\n- Check for numerical stability in the correlation result\n- Confirm that all operations match the task's exact metric",
